## Why This Is Asked
Tests proficiency designing fine-grained, scalable access controls for multi-tenant data lakes using ABAC, tags, and policy syntax.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tags and aws:PrincipalTag
- Resource policies with aws:RequestTag and stringEquals conditions
- S3 bucket prefixes per tenant and least privilege
- Testing cross-tenant access and tag revocation

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy (IAM role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Permission policy
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Bucket policy (alternative strict guard)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"s3:ExistingObjectTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tags and ensure no stale access remains?
- How would you integrate with CI to validate policies in a sandbox before prod?

## Why This Is Asked

This validates practical isolation in a shared data lake, ensuring least privilege and scalable onboarding. It also tests policy layering and auditing capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- IAM roles per tenant with strict resource boundaries
- Trust policies tied to the tenant app identity
- S3 prefix-scoped permissions with bucket or IAM policy conditions
- Onboarding automation for new tenants and centralized governance
- Cross-tenant test plans and auditing hooks

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake",
      "Condition": {"StringLike": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-<tid>/*"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake/tenant-<tid>/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "<tid>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::<account>:role/TenantRole-<tid>",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "<tid>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this model to support cross-region backups without cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you automate onboarding for new tenants without touching code (CI/CD policy issuance, audit log integration)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes real-world patterns for tenant isolation, scalable quotas, and DR in multi-tenant SaaS on AWS, combining IAM TBAC, API Gateway authorizers, S3 replication, and cross-region resilience.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tag-based access control (TBAC) with aws:PrincipalTag
- Lambda authorizers extracting tenantId from JWT
- S3 bucket policies enforcing tenant-scoped prefixes
- DynamoDB-based per-tenant leaky-bucket quotas
- Cross-region replication (S3) and Global Tables (DynamoDB)
- Route 53 failover for DR

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: Lambda authorizer outline
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const token = event.headers.Authorization;
  const tenantId = decodeJWT(token).tenantId;
  return { principalId: tenantId, policyDocument: { /* allow specific actions on tenant Artifacts */ }, context: { tenantId } };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you revoke a tenant's access immediately in a shared bucket?
- How would you observe and alert on quota exhaustion across regions?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical federation, per-service IAM scoping, and operational controls for external partner access. It requires concrete policy boundaries, token lifetimes, and revocation/audit mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- OIDC federation with API Gateway
- STS: AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity
- Per-service IAM roles and least privilege
- Trust policies and cross-account boundaries
- Token lifetimes and revocation workflows
- CloudTrail/audit trails and alerting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Trust policy for the role assumed via web identity
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"Federated": "arn:aws:iam::000000000000:oidc-provider/partner-idp"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"token_use": "id"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Per-service policy snippet (example for S3 access)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::tenant-<tenant_id>-data/*"]
    }
  ]
}
```

## Rollout Plan

- Integrate partner IdP with AWS IAM OIDC provider
- Create per-service roles and API Gateway authorizers
- Set TTLs to 3600s and enable automatic revocation
- Enable CloudTrail data events and alerting
- Onboard/offboard workflow and monitoring

